Let's denote the first term of the geometric sequence by \(a\) and the common ratio by \(r\). The \(n\)-th term of the sequence can be expressed as \(a \cdot r^{n-1}\). Given the 5th term is 11 and the 11th term is 5, we can write the following equations: \[
a \cdot r^4 = 11 \quad \text{(1)} \\
a \cdot r^{10} = 5 \quad \text{(2)}
\]

Dividing equation (2) by equation (1), we get: \[
\frac{a \cdot r^{10}}{a \cdot r^4} = \frac{5}{11} \implies r^6 = \frac{5}{11} \implies r = \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}
\]

Substitute \(r\) back into equation (1) to find \(a\): \[
a \cdot \left(\left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}\right)^4 = 11 \implies a \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{2/3} = 11 \implies a = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{2/3}
\]

The eighth term of the sequence is \(a \cdot r^7\). Substituting \(a\) and \(r\), we get: \[
a \cdot r^7 = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{2/3} \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{7/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11^2}{5^2}\right)^{1/3} \cdot \left(\frac{5^7}{11^7}\right)^{1/6} = 11 \cdot \left(\frac{11^2}{5^2}\right)^{1/3} \cdot \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{7/6}
\]

Simplifying the exponents, we have: \[
11 \cdot \frac{11^{2/3}}{5^{2/3}} \cdot \frac{5^{7/6}}{11^{7/6}} = 11 \cdot \frac{11^{2/3} \cdot 5^{7/6}}{5^{2/3} \cdot 11^{7/6}} = 11^{1 - 7/6} \cdot 5^{7/6 - 2/3} = 11^{-1/6} \cdot 5^{1/2} = 5^{1/2} \cdot 11^{-1/6} = \sqrt{5} \cdot \sqrt[6]{\frac{1}{11}}
\]

Therefore, the eighth term is: \[
\boxed{\sqrt[3]{5/11}}
\]

